A||||1||Smokin87||HELP...Still no brakes||||4||
Z||000000||Smokin87||04-01-2001||01:52 PM||||Someone please help me.<BR>i was having some brake problems, there was excessive pedel movement, so i changed the master cylinder.  after that all was ok, but i couldnt bleed the front brakes cause the valves were stripped.  Replaced one valve, and one caliper. drove it around after bleedint them, it was an inprovement, but not great.  the next day i bleed them again(figuring that the air would settle)  now i have NO brakes.  the pedle goes rite to the floor.  according to the repair manual, it says i could have a power brake malfuncion.  is there something i could do, or check for this.  or, is there something else that is wrong, that i could fix.<BR>thanks<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_copy.jpg">  <BR>"When man wanted to make the car faster...he invented the jet-plane."||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||04-01-2001||02:13 PM||||The new MC may be bad. You may not have all the air out. Tilted MC's often trap air in the pirmary section of the MC.<P>TAke the new MC off and bench bleed it again. Be very carefull to avoid getting air in it while reinstalling it. you have to be especially carefull with the rear MC opening. The rear opening is the pirmary. If you look at my drawings in the brake upgrade article you may understand why better. (I'mm have a new drawing for the brake service article that explains this next week.)<P>LEave the bench bleed stuff connected untill you are about to connect each line to it. You must have fluid in the MC at all times after the bench bleed. pinch the bleed lines and put the lid on after the bleed.<P>Another posibility is to jack the car untill the MC is level and bleed the rear brakes then the front. (The front circuit is less likely to trap air in the MC.) This can be very tricky and dangerous. parking nose down a hill can also be done but isn't any safer. Use extreme caution trying this method. The car must be blocked and/or chained to prevent it falling off the jacks.<P>Again, it is absolutly critical that the MC does not run out of fluid at any time. If it does then you have to start all over.||||reg||1
